  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLight showers across the area currently, should quickly\nscatter out over the next couple hours as the mid-level \nshortwave and associated large-scale forcing for ascent\ndissipates.\n\nA low-level jet over lower Michigan will fuel continues \nmoisture advection, which when coupled with the upper-level jet\nstream, should provide a more steadier stream of moderate rain\nthis evening into tonight across parts of Northwest\nPennsylvania. The latest 12Z HREF has about a 20-30% of >1\" of\nrain, mainly in Erie, PA. Flash flooding is not expected there\nbut certainly enough QPF for some minor, nuisance flooding.\n\nElsewhere, light, scattered showers expected again late tonight\nas the next mid-level shortwave (currently over eastern Iowa), \nmoves through. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An upper-level ridge gradually builds in from\nthe southwest through the next couple days. Surface high\npressure located to our southeast favors continued southwest\nadvection of a hot and humid airmass into our forecast area \nstarting Monday and especially Tuesday and Wednesday when Major\nHeatRisk impacts are expected. While highs are likely to be in\nthe low 90s on Tuesday and Wednesday humidity will contribute \nto heat indices in the mid 90s to low 100s. \n \nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nThe excessive heat and humidity this week will also contribute\nto building instability to the region, though weak forcing with\na low-amplitude trough to the north makes it difficult to\ndetermine when and where thunderstorms develop. If storms\ndevelop, moderate mid-level flow along the periphery of the\nbuilding ridge would favor severe weather, hence a broad day 3\nSPC marginal risk for Tuesday, despite the very low confidence \nin thunderstorm evolution. Wednesday should be similar before we\nget slightly better confidence in storm develop on Thursday\nahead an approaching cold front from the north.",
